#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536946} :activity/id #uuid "884db384-9de1-557a-84a4-3a3f0a422a85" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536946 :step/content {:assigned-problem/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536806 :multistep/context "![](images/2b/2bbc3e9ce241640af48af50c7d0e0c2c4120b0fc16a00fbc7837ecaeab6bbd9a.png)\n\n \n\nA function $f(x)$ is infinitely differentiable for all real values of its argument $x.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ and the tangent line to it at the point where $x=0$ are shown above. A few selected derivatives of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ are also given in the table below.\n\n| $n$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{(n)}(0)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nIn addition, the function $g$ is defined as \n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, it is known that the coefficients of the terms of the Maclaurin series for $g(x)$ form a convergent alternating series $S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :multistep/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536916} :multistep/id #uuid "2175db54-7935-51ff-8029-dacec9980dc4" :multistep/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536809 :question/id #uuid "0fac5cd1-6321-5b31-b6c2-3285240579dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-129775"} :step/id #uuid "259f7312-1450-5437-8b0c-e3169741fe4f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536856 :question/id #uuid "8d3a5d6c-af68-5a0e-b08b-800ce9329d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129769"} :step/id #uuid "2c34a7a0-14a6-5a48-8af3-6b82adf7a7b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536880 :question/id #uuid "ca513941-537a-5b56-95d2-50fbfa2cbedd" :question/math-academy-id "q-129770"} :step/id #uuid "31a6c27d-2709-5a68-b854-2de759080b64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536812 :question/id #uuid "13fe64